@* MultiSelect's Overview, written by hand after Combobox's, whose script it shares: choosing
   several (previewed in each mode), at least one with ids for values, enum options with icons,
   sizes and states, and its own words. Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<Heading Level="2">Usage</Heading>
<Code Block Language="razor">@Usage</Code>
<TextBlock>A multi-select is a field where the user picks several options from a long list, typing to narrow it. The chosen options show as chips in the field.</TextBlock>
<TextBlock>For a short list, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/checkbox-group">CheckboxGroup</Anchor>, which shows every option at once. For one choice, see <Anchor Href="docs/components/combobox">Combobox</Anchor> or <Anchor Href="docs/components/select">Select</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Choosing several</Heading>
<TextBlock>The field's value is the collection of chosen options. Set <Code>@@bind-Value</Code> to a collection and pass the options to <Code>Items</Code>. Typing filters the list, ignoring case and accents, and the list stays open as the user chooses.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="multi-select-chips" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The collection can be an array, a <Code>List&lt;T&gt;</Code>, a <Code>HashSet&lt;T&gt;</Code>, or an interface they implement.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Each choice posts as a repeated form field.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>An empty choice posts nothing. On <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">static pages</Anchor> the property is then null, or the model isn't bound at all. So set defaults only when the request isn't this form's post, as this example does (<Anchor Href="docs/troubleshooting/empty-selection">why</Anchor>).</ListItem>
    <ListItem><Code>[MaxLength]</Code> limits how many the user can choose. The validator checks it, not the browser.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">At least one, with a placeholder</Heading>
<TextBlock>A required multi-select starts empty, showing its placeholder, and needs at least one choice before the form posts. Add <Code>[Required]</Code> and <Code>[MinLength(1)]</Code> together to the property, and set <Code>Placeholder</Code>. When the values are ids, set <Code>ItemText</Code> to name each option.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="multi-select-reviewers" />
<List>
    <ListItem>The browser checks <Code>[Required]</Code> before the form posts.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On static pages, the server's <Code>[Required]</Code> catches an empty choice, because the property is null.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>On interactive pages, <Code>[MinLength(1)]</Code> catches it after the last choice is removed, because the property is an empty list.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>A form post can contain values that aren't among the options, and they reach the model. This example checks them on the server. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Typed values</Heading>
<TextBlock>To take values that aren't among the options, such as new labels or email addresses, set <Code>AllowCustom</Code>. <Code>Items</Code> become suggestions, and can be left out. It needs a collection of strings.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="multi-select-recipients" />
<List>
    <ListItem>Enter adds the typed text, unless the user moved to an option with the arrow keys. A comma or semicolon adds the text before it, and so does leaving the field.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Pasting splits the text on commas, semicolons and line breaks, and adds each piece.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Text that matches an option, ignoring case, chooses that option. A value already chosen isn't added again.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>Check the values with the model's validation, as this example's <Code>[Emails]</Code> attribute does. It runs on the server for static pages and on interactive ones as the form submits.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>New values need JavaScript. With it off, the field is a plain list of the options.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Enum options with icons</Heading>
<TextBlock>Each option in the list can show markup, such as an icon beside an enum value's name. Set <Code>ItemTemplate</Code> to the markup, and pass the enum's values to <Code>Items</Code>: <Code>Enum.GetValues&lt;IssueLabel&gt;()</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="multi-select-enum-icons" />
<TextBlock>The chips, typing, and the plain list with JavaScript off use the option's text, not the template. The text is <Code>ItemText</Code>, or else the enum's <Code>[Display(Name)]</Code> or its name. The template writes its own text, so show the same text there.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Groups</Heading>
<TextBlock>To list the options under headings, set <Code>ItemGroup</Code>, as on <Anchor Href="docs/components/combobox#groups">Combobox</Anchor>: groups in the order they first appear, and a group with no match hidden as the user types. An enum's options group by their <Code>[Display(GroupName)]</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>The size lines the field up with buttons and other fields; set <Code>Size</Code> to choose one. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="multi-select-states" />
<TextBlock>HTML has no read-only select, so a read-only MultiSelect shows its choices in a read-only text field and posts them from hidden inputs. Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Labels and localization</Heading>
<TextBlock>The field shows text when nothing matches, and its buttons have names for screen readers. To set them for the whole app, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/text">Text and other languages</Anchor>.</TextBlock>
<Prose Class="site-table ib-scroll">
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Sets</th><th>Default</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><Code>EmptyText</Code></td><td>The text when no option matches</td><td>"No matches"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>ToggleLabel</Code></td><td>The name of the button that opens the list</td><td>"Show options"</td></tr>
            <tr><td><Code>RemoveLabel</Code></td><td>The start of each chip's remove button name, as in "Remove Blazor"</td><td>"Remove"</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</Prose>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

<Heading Level="2">Parameters</Heading>
<ParameterTable ApiComponent="ApiComponent" ApiHref="@Registry.ApiPageHref(Component)" Context="Context" Compact />

<OverviewSeeAlso Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public RegistryComponent Component { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiComponent ApiComponent { get; set; } = null!;

    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public ApiPageContext Context { get; set; } = null!;

    private const string Usage = """
        @* Strings: each is its own text. *@
        <MultiSelect @bind-Value="Model!.Skills" Items="AllSkills" Placeholder="Add skills" />

        @* Ids, named for people. *@
        <MultiSelect @bind-Value="Model!.ReviewerIds" Items="People.Keys"
                     ItemText="id => People.GetValueOrDefault(id, id.ToString())" />

        @* An enum's values. *@
        <MultiSelect @bind-Value="Model!.Days" Items="Enum.GetValues<DayOfWeek>()" />
        """;
}
